From 047d16607997830d43bc7a4d525c298b17e2f4c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] kk: build a graphics pipeline's Metal render pipeline state

 on a worker thread

MESA_KK_ASYNC_PIPELINES=1 or =2 (read once at device creation, unset or 0
keeps the old behaviour) makes vkCreateGraphicsPipelines stop waiting for
Metal. The NIR->MSL translation is still done at creation (it is CPU work
and feeds the pipeline cache), but the expensive part - building the two
MTLLibraries and the MTLRenderPipelineState - is handed to a small
util_queue worker pool. Only the worker thread ever calls Metal for it; a
recording or submission thread never does, in either mode.

A pipeline whose render pipeline state is not ready cannot be bound, so a
draw that would bind it cannot be recorded yet. The two modes differ in what
happens to such a draw:

  1  the draw is skipped and the command buffer is re-recorded at
     submission, where the queue waits for the compile and records the
     draw. Nothing is lost, but a 60 s compile still stalls the frame that
     first submits a draw of that pipeline.

  2  the draw is dropped and never waited for; the command buffer is not
     re-recorded for it. The pipeline is used by later command buffers once
     the state is published: the draws of the first frames that used it are
     missing (pop-in), and the recording and submission threads run
     unimpeded. Mode 2 is what to use when a stall is worse than a few
     missing draws; it also drops the draw when Metal failed to build the
     state (setting VK_ERROR_INVALID_SHADER_NV on that command buffer),
     since it has no replay that could retry the compile.

A dropped draw is decided before anything is flushed or encoded, so it
leaves the encoder, the flushed dynamic state, the occlusion query results
(BEGIN/END still brackets the dropped draws, so they count zero) and the
transform feedback counters (dropped draws write none) exactly as if it had
not been recorded.

Programs with a sampler min/max emulating companion (info.minmax_sets) stay
synchronous: one of their two programs is compiled on first use, so at
creation there is no MSL to build a state from. Compute pipelines are
untouched. A program that is already in the pipeline cache does not go
through this path either (vk_pipeline_precompile_shader returns before
ops->compile on a cache hit).

The job owns copies of both stages' MSL and of the render pipeline
descriptor's inputs, so the worker never reads the program: the MSL can be
released at creation, and a program can be destroyed while its compile runs
(kk_shader_destroy drops or waits for the job). The state is published with
a compare-exchange and the status with a release store the fence is
signalled after, so a draw that sees it ready observes the handle.

MESA_KK_DEBUG=async logs queued compiles and dropped draws.

